<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="production"
      updated="August 4, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"fleet-shape", "The shape of a production fleet"},
        {"phased-rollout", "Roll out in phases"},
        {"worked-examples", "Two worked examples"},
        {"go-live-checklist", "Go-live checklist"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="production">
        <:dek>
          The
          <.link navigate={~p"/docs/quickstart"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
            quickstart
          </.link>
          installed one runner on one host. Production adds many hosts, real consequences, and a
          security review that asks exactly how emisar stops an unsafe call.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        None of this needs much configuration — the defaults are already conservative (<.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/policies-and-approvals"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >policies &amp; approvals</.link>
        states them). Going to production is mostly deciding <em>where</em>
        to tighten, and doing it once.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="fleet-shape">The shape of a production fleet</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Make two decisions first: how to group runners, and which people can reach each group.
        An agent key inherits its owner's scope, so when you limit a person you limit their
        agents too. Everything else on this page builds on these two decisions.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Installing runners — on a <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/host-install"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >host</.link>, in a <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/containers"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >container</.link>, on a
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/kubernetes"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">Kubernetes</.link>
        or
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/nomad"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">Nomad</.link>
        node — has its own pages under Deploy on.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Group by tier, not by host.</strong>
            A
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/runner-fleet" <> "#groups-labels"}
              class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
            >group</.link>
            is what policy, runbooks, member scopes, and agents all target. Name it for a fleet
            role — <code class="font-mono">web</code>, <code class="font-mono">edge</code>, <code class="font-mono">db-prod</code>,
            <code class="font-mono">cache</code>
            — never one machine. The installer defaults the group to the hostname; change it
            before you add a second host. <code class="font-mono">web-01</code>
            is a label, not a group.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Labels for everything else.</strong>
            Free-form <code class="font-mono">key=value</code>
            pairs — <code class="font-mono">region=us-east-1</code>, <code class="font-mono">environment=prod</code>,
            <code class="font-mono">service=checkout</code>
            — for filtering and searching the fleet without redrawing your groups. (Policy targets a
            runner, a group, or the account — not labels.)
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Keep policy and access aligned.</strong>
            Policy controls what a runner runs; access controls who reaches it. A locked database
            tier gets both — a strict ruleset, scoped to the DBAs and their agents.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p class="mt-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Use this example to shape your runner fleet:
      </p>
      <div class="mt-3 divide-y divide-zinc-900 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-zinc-800">
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-1 p-4 sm:grid-cols-[8rem_1fr]">
          <div class="font-mono text-sm text-brand-300">web</div>
          <div class="text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            Ten hosts use <code class="font-mono">nginx</code>, <code class="font-mono">linux-core</code>, and <code class="font-mono">debugging</code>.
            Policy allows reads and requires one approval for a restart.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-1 p-4 sm:grid-cols-[8rem_1fr]">
          <div class="font-mono text-sm text-amber-300">db-prod</div>
          <div class="text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            Three hosts use <code class="font-mono">postgres</code>
            and <code class="font-mono">linux-core</code>.
            A targeted ruleset denies <.docs_risk tier="critical" />
            and requires two approvals for <.docs_risk tier="high" />. Scope only the DBA
            team.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-1 p-4 sm:grid-cols-[8rem_1fr]">
          <div class="font-mono text-sm text-zinc-300">staging</div>
          <div class="text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            Two hosts use the full catalog, including the <code class="font-mono">shell</code>
            break-glass pack. Its looser policy lets an agent iterate on a fix, and it cannot
            reach a production host.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <.docs_h2 id="phased-rollout">Roll out in phases</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Each phase is reversible and safe to stop after. No phase leaves the fleet more open than
        the previous one.
      </p>
      <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li>
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Pilot on one non-prod host.</strong>
          Install, watch it connect, run a read, then open the row from <a
            href={~p"/app/audit"}
            class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
          >Audit</a>. Prove the whole path before it touches anything that matters.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Configure the policy.</strong>
          Decide what each risk tier can do across the fleet. Keep policy strict where an action
          can take something down, and relax it only after an explicit decision. The editor will not
          let a higher tier be more permissive than a lower one. See <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/policies-and-approvals"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >policies &amp; approvals</.link>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Add approvals where they matter.</strong>
          For anything that can drop a service or lose data, turn on four-eyes — two different
          approvers, self-approval off. Approvers get an email with the request.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Scope people and agents.</strong>
          Give each member only the runners or groups they own — their agents' keys carry the
          same limit. Out-of-scope hosts are invisible to them.
          See <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/teams-and-access" <> "#runner-scopes"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >per-member runner scopes</.link>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Lock the sensitive groups.</strong>
          Add a targeted ruleset for your production tiers. A ruleset <em>replaces</em>
          the default for its target. The most specific rule wins: runner, then group, then default.
          The <code class="font-mono">db-prod</code>
          group can deny <.docs_risk tier="critical" /> and require two approvals for
          <.docs_risk tier="high" />. The rest of the fleet
          can keep the default.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Connect identity.</strong>
          Require MFA account-wide. Add
          <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/sso"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >SSO</.link>
          so people sign in through your identity provider. <.plan_note tier={:team} /> Add
          <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/scim"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >SCIM</.link>
          so a suspension in your directory ends sessions and revokes keys in emisar — no
          manual checklist. <.plan_note tier={:enterprise} />
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Connect your SIEM.</strong>
          Mint an
          <a
            href={~p"/app/audit/export"}
            class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
          >audit-export token</a>
          — the export is a pull API, so configure a collector to poll the NDJSON endpoint.
          See <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/audit-and-siem"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >audit &amp; SIEM</.link>. <.plan_note tier={:team} />
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Before widening the rollout, prove the whole path once: run one low-risk read-only action
        on one non-critical runner, confirm it succeeds and appears in run history, and confirm
        your collector sends it to the SIEM. Repeat that check after every runner upgrade and
        pack rollout. Day-to-day signals — connectivity, run states, waiting approvals, pack
        trust, poller health — live with their owner pages under Operate.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="worked-examples">Two worked examples</.docs_h2>

      <div class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-zinc-800 bg-zinc-950/60 p-5">
        <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-zinc-100">
          An AI agent that reads the web tier and restarts behind approval
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
          <strong class="text-zinc-300">Goal:</strong>
          Let the incident agent investigate nginx without waiting. Require a person to approve
          each restart.
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
            <span>
              <strong class="text-zinc-300">Fleet:</strong>
              the ten web hosts share group <code class="font-mono">web</code>, packs
              <code class="font-mono">nginx</code>
              + <code class="font-mono">linux-core</code>
              + <code class="font-mono">debugging</code>.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
            <span>
              <strong class="text-zinc-300">Policy:</strong>
              The defaults already fit. Low-risk and medium-risk log or status reads run
              immediately. The high-risk <code class="font-mono">linux.systemctl_restart</code>
              action requires approval — no override needed.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
            <span>
              <strong class="text-zinc-300">Access:</strong>
              Scope the operator to the <code class="font-mono">web</code>
              group — they cannot reach another tier, and their agent key inherits the same
              scope.
            </span>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
          <strong class="text-zinc-300">Result:</strong>
          The agent runs <code class="font-mono">nginx.error_tail</code>
          immediately. A <code class="font-mono">linux.systemctl_restart</code>
          call waits for the on-call operator. The operator sees the host, exact arguments, and
          agent reason before approval. Each completed or gated run records its client, key, and
          reason in the audit.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-zinc-800 bg-zinc-950/60 p-5">
        <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-zinc-100">
          A destructive action on db-prod, done safely
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
          <strong class="text-zinc-300">Goal:</strong>
          Allow terminating a stuck Postgres backend on <code class="font-mono">db-prod</code>
          only after two people approve.
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
            <span>
              <strong class="text-zinc-300">Fleet:</strong>
              group <code class="font-mono">db-prod</code>, three hosts, packs
              <code class="font-mono">postgres</code>
              + <code class="font-mono">linux-core</code>.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
            <span>
              <strong class="text-zinc-300">Policy + approvals:</strong>
              a targeted ruleset for the group — <.docs_risk tier="critical" /> denied,
              <.docs_risk tier="high" />
              require approval — with four-eyes on: two approvers, self-approval off.
            </span>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
          <strong class="text-zinc-300">The flow:</strong>
          A DBA agent calls <code class="font-mono">postgres.terminate_backend</code>
          with a reason. Policy holds the run for approval. Two other operators approve it. Because
          self-approval is off, the requester cannot be one of them. The runner executes the approved
          run. The audit records the requester, both approvers, and redacted command. No one drops a
          <code class="font-mono">db-prod</code>
          connection alone, and every step is recorded.
        </p>
      </div>

      <.docs_h2 id="go-live-checklist">Go-live checklist</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The defaults ship safe, so this list is only the decisions you made on this page —
        walk it before you point production at emisar. A security reviewer will ask for the
        same things:
      </p>
      <.docs_callout kind={:note} icon="hero-shield-check">
        <p class="text-base font-semibold text-zinc-100">Every account</p>
        <ul class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-300">
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <.icon name="hero-check" class="mt-1 h-4 w-4 flex-none text-brand-400" />
            <span>Group runners by tier. Give every production group its own locked ruleset.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <.icon name="hero-check" class="mt-1 h-4 w-4 flex-none text-brand-400" />
            <span>Four-eyes is on for any group that can lose data or drop a service.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <.icon name="hero-check" class="mt-1 h-4 w-4 flex-none text-brand-400" />
            <span>Members are scoped to the hosts they own — agent keys inherit that scope, so
            nothing is account-wide by accident.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <.icon name="hero-check" class="mt-1 h-4 w-4 flex-none text-brand-400" />
            <span>Require MFA account-wide.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <.icon name="hero-check" class="mt-1 h-4 w-4 flex-none text-brand-400" />
            <span>Pack secrets live in <code class="font-mono">runner.env</code>
            + <code class="font-mono">inherit_env</code>, never in call arguments.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <.icon name="hero-check" class="mt-1 h-4 w-4 flex-none text-brand-400" />
            <span>The <code class="font-mono">shell</code>
            break-glass pack is on no production runner.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <.icon name="hero-check" class="mt-1 h-4 w-4 flex-none text-brand-400" />
            <span>Signed dispatch is enforced wherever you must be able to prove which bridge sent
            a request. A customer-authorized bridge signs each request with a key the control
            plane never holds.</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-5 text-base font-semibold text-zinc-100">On Team, add</p>
        <ul class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-300">
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <.icon name="hero-check" class="mt-1 h-4 w-4 flex-none text-brand-400" />
            <span>Configure SSO so people sign in through your identity provider.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <.icon name="hero-check" class="mt-1 h-4 w-4 flex-none text-brand-400" />
            <span>Poll the audit export API with an <code class="font-mono">audit-export</code>
            token. Confirm that its cursor advances. Alert on policy changes and admission
            blocks.</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-5 text-base font-semibold text-zinc-100">
          On Enterprise, add — on top of everything in Team
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-300">
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <.icon name="hero-check" class="mt-1 h-4 w-4 flex-none text-brand-400" />
            <span>Configure SCIM so a suspension in your directory ends sessions and revokes keys in
            emisar.</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_callout kind={:tip} title="On Enterprise, you have help with this">
        Enterprise includes a security and procurement review, a fleet-specific rollout plan, and
        design-partner support. Tell us what you run, and we can help you deploy it. See
        <.link href={~p"/pricing"} class="font-medium text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
          plans
        </.link>
        or email <a
          href={EmisarWeb.MailTo.sales()}
          class="font-medium text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >sales@emisar.dev</a>.
      </.docs_callout>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
